      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village once thrived along Old Mine Road, a 104‑mile Dutch‑built route linking farms to markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/walpack-center-new-jersey-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1965 the Tocks Island Dam Project forced residents to abandon homes via eminent domain, ending the farming community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/walpack-center-new-jersey-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Eleven historic 19th‑century buildings, including a post office and church, survive and are protected within the 16‑acre historic district.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        13,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/walpack-center-new-jersey-ghost-town/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Walpack Historical Society works with the National Park Service to restore these structures, keeping the village’s Victorian and Italianate architecture alive.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/walpack-center-new-jersey-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Wallpack Center sits in the Flat Brook Valley, 6.7 miles west of Branchville, now part of the Delaware Water Gap National Recreation Area.",
